<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest</AnalysisLevel>
    <NeutralLanguage>en-US</NeutralLanguage>
    <InvariantGlobalization>true</InvariantGlobalization>
    <RootNamespace>$(MSBuildProjectName)</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>$(MSBuildProjectName)</AssemblyName>
    <NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
    <NuGetAuditMode>direct</NuGetAuditMode>
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    ===========================================================================================
    THE Kiln.slnx DEBUG FOOT-GUN, AND ITS FIX. Applies only to the EXPERIMENTAL Weft pilot
    (-p:KilnWeftPilot=true); an ordinary kiln build never evaluates any of it.
    ===========================================================================================

    The Weft projects cannot be members of Kiln.slnx: WeftRepoRoot is a build-time property and a
    solution file has to name its projects statically. When MSBuild builds a solution it runs every
    ProjectReference through AssignProjectConfiguration, which maps in-solution references through
    the solution's configuration table. An OUT-of-solution reference has no row in that table, and
    MSBuild's default outside Visual Studio (ShouldUnsetParentConfigurationAndPlatform = true) is
    to STRIP Configuration/Platform from the properties passed down — so the referenced project
    builds at its own default, which is Debug.

    Measured, before this file changed (webhost1, kiln 74fb8d7, kx-runs/10-slnx-footgun.log):

      dotnet build -c Release -p:KilnWeftPilot=true ... Kiln.slnx
        Kiln    -> src/Kiln/bin/Release/net10.0/Kiln.dll        <- Release
        Weft.Ir -> src/Weft.Ir/bin/DEBUG/net10.0/Weft.Ir.dll    <- unoptimized, silently
      and the Weft.Ir.dll copied next to Kiln.dll md5-matched the Debug build exactly.

    Anyone benchmarking the pilot that way measures an unoptimized Weft.

    WHY THIS LIVES HERE AND NOT IN Kiln.csproj. It was tried in Kiln.csproj first, and that fixed
    only half the problem — measured, not assumed (kx-runs/11-slnx-diagnostic.log): src/Kiln got
    Release Weft, while tests/Kiln.Tests, bench/Kiln.Benchmarks and samples/Kiln.Capture all still
    copied the DEBUG Weft, because each of them resolves Weft TRANSITIVELY through Kiln and runs
    its own AssignProjectConfiguration, which knew nothing about the property. The benchmark
    project was among the ones still getting Debug — i.e. the half-fix would have left the exact
    measurement this matters for still broken. Directory.Build.props is the only place that
    reaches every project that can transitively see Weft.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(KilnWeftPilot)' == 'true'">
    <ShouldUnsetParentConfigurationAndPlatform>false</ShouldUnsetParentConfigurationAndPlatform>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    BUILD-TIME GUARD. The property above fixes the cause; this proves it stayed fixed on every
    build, from every entry point (solution, project file, IDE, a future MSBuild whose defaults
    differ), in every project — not just the one that declares the references.

    It inspects @(ReferenceCopyLocalPaths), which is the set of files this project is about to copy
    into its own output directory, with their SOURCE paths. That is deliberately the strongest
    available signal: it is the actual Weft.dll the built program will load, direct or transitive,
    rather than a property whose propagation we would otherwise be taking on trust.

    A README line does not survive; a build error cannot be forgotten.
  -->
  <Target Name="KilnWeftPilotConfigurationGuard"
          AfterTargets="ResolveAssemblyReferences"
          Condition="'$(KilnWeftPilot)' == 'true' and '$(Configuration)' != ''">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_KilnWeftCopied Include="@(ReferenceCopyLocalPaths)"
                       Condition="'%(Extension)' == '.dll' and $([System.String]::Copy('%(Filename)').StartsWith('Weft.'))" />
      <_KilnWeftWrongConfig Include="@(_KilnWeftCopied)"
                            Condition="!$([System.String]::Copy('%(Identity)').Replace('\', '/').Contains('/bin/$(Configuration)/'))" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <Error Condition="'@(_KilnWeftWrongConfig)' != ''"
           Text="$(MSBuildProjectName) is building '$(Configuration)' but is about to copy Weft assemblies built in a DIFFERENT configuration:%0A  @(_KilnWeftWrongConfig, '%0A  ')%0AThis is the Kiln.slnx foot-gun (see the ShouldUnsetParentConfigurationAndPlatform comment in Directory.Build.props). A Release Kiln linked against a Debug Weft benchmarks as an unoptimized Weft and the number is meaningless. Fix the configuration flow before trusting any measurement." />
  </Target>
</Project>
